<http://example.org/multimodal-taxonomy#BackgroundKnowledge> <http://example.org/multimodal-taxonomy#promptExtractionText> "Extract Background Knowledge elements from the image. Background Knowledge includes: cultural references, historical context, social knowledge, domain-specific information, contextual cues, and background information. For each knowledge element found, provide: 1) A descriptive instance_name (e.g., 'cultural_reference', 'historical_context'), 2) A natural language label (e.g., 'the cultural reference', 'the historical context'), 3) A brief description of the knowledge or context required to understand the element. Focus on information that viewers need to know to fully comprehend the meme. Example output format: {\"instance_name\": \"cultural_reference\", \"label\": \"the cultural reference\", \"description\": \"Reference to the 'This is fine' dog meme, requiring knowledge of internet culture to understand the irony\"}"@en .
<http://example.org/multimodal-taxonomy#Metadata> <http://example.org/multimodal-taxonomy#promptExtractionText> "Extract Metadata elements from the image. Metadata includes: technical information, contextual descriptions, descriptive attributes, metadata properties, and informational elements. For each metadata element found, provide: 1) A descriptive instance_name (e.g., 'image_format', 'creation_context'), 2) A natural language label (e.g., 'the image format', 'the creation context'), 3) A brief description of the metadata information. Focus on descriptive and technical information about the meme itself. Example output format: {\"instance_name\": \"image_format\", \"label\": \"the image format\", \"description\": \"Digital image format with high contrast and clear visual elements typical of internet memes\"}"@en .
<http://example.org/multimodal-taxonomy#Modal> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#label> "Modal"@en .
<http://example.org/multimodal-taxonomy#Semi-transparentOverlay> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#subClassOf> <http://example.org/multimodal-taxonomy#DirectSuperimposition> .
_:nbf72ac90c67a4388a516a8fcbbb7ae46b25 <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#unionOf> _:nbf72ac90c67a4388a516a8fcbbb7ae46b26 .
<http://w3c.org/memes-ontology/general/01348#isis_strip_club_meme_concept> <http://example.org/multimodal-taxonomy#relatedTo> <http://w3c.org/memes-ontology/general/01348#sheep_line_scene> .
<http://w3c.org/memes-ontology/general/01348#isis_strip_club_meme_concept> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#comment> "A meme format that uses the juxtaposition of an image of sheep with their rear ends facing forward (often called 'sheep parade' or 'sheep butt' meme) with a caption implying a 'strip club' setting, and overlays it with images of men in traditional head coverings reacting with exaggerated shock or amusement. The concept relies on the absurdity of the visual juxtaposition and the implication of sexualized or inappropriate activity, often targeting specific cultural or religious groups through stereotypes."@en .
_:nbf72ac90c67a4388a516a8fcbbb7ae46b91 <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#unionOf> _:nbf72ac90c67a4388a516a8fcbbb7ae46b92 .
